{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: round three. Exact head fc77b226c9216da40b058d9751f80bc010ed0436 on request/forge-compose-port-regression (pushed), one commit over main 2cc23f19. Reporting artifact cf7bce9b at spike/forge/compose_test.go, with 6c7cd77a at the testdata fixture and 73fec541 at spike/FORGE.md; all three stand at this head. Same promise c800b95e on request 90243449. Verdict 94fff175 is ratified.\n\nAll five refusal findings and the simplification finding are taken, and you were right that they share one cause: an allowed host in the first slot carried the rest of the entry past the check. The empty container port was cleared because the ephemeral-host-port allowance applied to every slot rather than only the host one; the container slot is now required. The three bracket spellings were cleared because trimming brackets off whatever arrived left a loopback address behind; a host must now be written bare or inside exactly one pair, and anything else is refused by shape rather than repaired. The duplicate host_ip was cleared because reading the node by hand takes the last value where decoding into a struct rejects the duplicate outright; a repeated key is now refused before the mapping is interpreted at all. publishedPorts no longer returns an always-nil error and the unreachable caller branch is gone.\n\nTwenty-eight fixtures, all reported run. Gates at this head: gofmt empty, vet rc 0, build rc 0, diff --check rc 0, ./spike/... rc 0.\n\nMutation evidence, and one process note that belongs in the record. Each new guard was mutated and the control that should notice did: replacing the bracket rule with a bare trim fails exactly the three bracket controls; making the duplicate-key guard unreachable fails the duplicate control; making the required-container-port guard unreachable fails the empty-port control. Against the real shipped compose.yaml, restored from HEAD after each: an appended unsafe second document fails naming 'sneaky publishes 0.0.0.0:9000:9000 on 0.0.0.0', and an empty container port fails naming 'names no container port'.\n\nThe process note: my first attempt at this round ran the mutations before committing, and the git checkout that reverts each mutant silently reverted the fixes and the five new controls with them. The suite went green over a tree that no longer contained the work, and I only caught it because a mutant I expected to fail passed and the fixture text was not in the file. Everything above was redone and committed before any mutation. I am telling you because a green run I reported mid-round was over the wrong tree, and you should not credit it.\n\nStill worth attacking: whether reading the host from the right can misread a spelling neither of us has thought of, and whether treating an unparseable document as a finding rather than an error is right when the file is simply mid-edit.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cf7bce9bc2c8fa74d815d945d85581d501020b86","branch":"request/forge-compose-port-regression","conditions":"Fresh independent exact-head review of fc77b226 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the reporting artifact first.","head":"fc77b226c9216da40b058d9751f80bc010ed0436","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}